{"id":"7f32cbff-4d4e-4ed8-bbe1-7afe19c324e2","object":"chat.completion","created":1785005864,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    req_syms = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    node_map = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n    parent = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        parent[n[\\\"id\\\"]] = n[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            root_id = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n            break\\n    retained = set()\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if not n[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            retained.add(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    retained.add(root_id)\\n    changed = True\\n    while changed:\\n        changed = False\\n        for nid in list(retained):\\n            p = parent[nid]\\n            if p is not None and p not in retained:\\n                retained.add(p)\\n                changed = True\\n    while True:\\n        uncovered = [sym for sym in req_syms if not any(node_map[nid][\\\"symbol\\\"] == sym for nid in retained)]\\n        if not uncovered:\\n            break\\n        sym = uncovered[0]\\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\\\"symbol\\\"] == sym and n[\\\"id\\\"] not in retained]\\n        if not candidates:\\n            break\\n        candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[\\\"utility\\\"], x[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n        chosen = candidates[0]\\n        node = chosen[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        while node is not None:\\n            if node not in retained:\\n                retained.add(node)\\n            node = parent[node]\\n    all_ids = set(n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes)\\n    removed = all_ids - retained\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": sorted(removed),\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": sorted(retained)\\n    }\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01 based on the description: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" with bounded objective: Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\n\nGiven the payload structure: nodes array with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility; plus required_symbols list. The task is to determine which nodes to remove (removed_node_ids) and which to retain (retained_node_ids) such that the resulting tree (after removals) still satisfies the required symbols? Also \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" suggests maybe we need to consider some constraints.\n\nFrom the fixture: nodes form a tree with parent references. Some nodes are removable (true/false). Required symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]. So we must keep nodes that have symbols \"root\" or \"s1\"? But also need to maintain tree structure: if we remove a node, its children are also removed? Or we can attach children to ancestors? The concept of \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" might be about allowed operations: deletion and replacement. In tree reduction, usually you can delete a node and attach its children to its parent, or replace a node with one of its subtrees. The objective is to reduce the tree (remove nodes) while preserving the required symbols (interestingness oracle). The nodes have utility values, but not sure if used.\n\nThe output schema requires \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\". So we need to decide which nodes to remove and which to retain. The fixture examples: each case has same structure. We need to infer the algorithm.\n\nGiven \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" – maybe we have a context-free grammar where each symbol can have certain replacements. But the payload does not include grammar rules. Perhaps it's implicit: the tree is a parse tree, and we can delete nodes that are not required for the grammar's start symbol? But we only have required_symbols list.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's about \"minimum reduction\" such that all required symbols are still present, and we can only remove nodes that are marked removable, and when removing a node, we must replace it with something? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". Could be a known problem: given a tree with labels, we want to reduce it by deleting nodes (if removable) or replacing subtrees while preserving a set of \"interesting\" symbols (required). But we need to implement solve(case) without external info.\n\nLet's analyze the fixtures to deduce expected behavior.\n\nCase 0: nodes:\nroot (depth0, not removable)\nn1 (depth1, parent root, s1, not removable, utility 0.8757)\nn2 (depth1, parent root, s2, removable, utility 0.5466)\nn3 (depth1, parent root, s0, not removable, utility 0.4360)\nn4 (depth2, parent n2, s1, removable, utility 0.6655)\nn5 (depth2, parent n3, s2, not removable, utility 0.7014)\nn6 (depth2, parent n1, s0, removable, utility 0.1950)\n\nRequired symbols: root, s1. So we need to keep at least one node with symbol root and at least one with symbol s1. root is present (not removable). s1 present at n1 (not removable) and n4 (removable). So we can keep n1 to satisfy s1. Also need to keep tree connectivity? If we delete n2, what happens to its child n4? If we delete n2, n4 would be orphaned unless reattached. The reduce operation might allow reattaching children to the parent of the deleted node. That would be a \"replacement\" where the deleted node is effectively removed and its children become children of the grandparent. Similar to grammar production: if we have A -> B C, and we delete A, we might attach B and C to the parent of A, but that changes structure. Or we could replace A with one of its children (e.g., A replaced by B). The description \"deletion and replacement candidates\" suggests both.\n\nBut we need output: removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. Probably we need to decide a set of nodes to remove such that after removal (and possibly restructuring), the tree still satisfies that all required symbols are present, and maybe other constraints.\n\nMaybe the task is: for each node marked removable, you can choose to delete it. You cannot delete non-removable nodes. For each removable node, you can either keep it or delete it. Deletion of a node may remove its subtree unless its children are reattached. But there's no info on reattachment rules.\n\nAnother idea: This might be a \"tree pruning\" problem where we want to keep only nodes that are on paths to required symbols, and we can prune branches that don't contribute. In the tree, required symbols are \"root\" and \"s1\". Nodes with symbol root: only root. With symbol s1: n1 and n4. So we must keep root and at least one s1. The minimal tree that includes root and a s1 could be keeping root, n1 (since n1 is child of root and has s1). Then we can remove n2, n3, n4, n5, n6. But n2 is removable, n3 is not removable? n3 is not removable (false), so we cannot remove n3. That's a problem. So we cannot remove non-removable nodes. Thus we must retain n3 even though its symbol s0 is not required. So the reduction must respect removable flags.\n\nThus the decision is: for each removable node, can we delete it? But we must also consider that deleting a node might affect the ability to satisfy required symbols if that node's subtree contains required symbols. For example, n4 has s1 (required). If we delete n2, n4 would be orphaned. But maybe we can reattach n4 to root directly? The problem may allow \"replacement\" where you can replace a node with one of its children, effectively moving the child up a level. So we could replace n2 with n4, then n4 becomes child of root with depth 1. That would preserve the required s1. Or we could delete n2 and n4? But we need an s1, so we must keep at least one s1 node.\n\nGiven we have n1 (s1, non-removable), we can safely delete both n2 and n4 (if allowed) because n1 already provides s1. But n2 is removable, n4 is removable, so we can delete them. However, we also need to consider n3 (non-removable) and n5 (non-removable) and n6 (removable). n5 is s2 (not required), n6 is s0 (not required). n3 is s0 (not required). So we can delete n6, but keep n3 and n5 because they are not removable.\n\nThus possible output: remove n2, n4, n6; retain root, n1, n3, n5. That seems plausible.\n\nBut is there any rule about tree structure after deletion? If we delete n2, its child n4 is also deleted (since we removed n4 explicitly). If we delete n2 but keep n4, that would break tree. So either we delete both or keep both and possibly reattach. Since n4 is removable, we can delete it together.\n\nCheck case 0: Does the fixture provide expected output? No, but we can infer from multiple cases. Let's look at case 1 and case 2 structure similar.\n\nCase 1: same symbols, same removable flags; only utility changes. So algorithm likely deterministic based on symbols and removable flags, not utility. So output should be same for all three? But utility might influence if we have choices? Possibly we want to maximize total utility after reduction? The objective description: \"bounded objective: Reduce ... while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" Bounded objective might mean minimize some cost or maximize utility? But output doesn't include utility. It only requires removed/retained ids. So maybe the algorithm is to remove all removable nodes that are not necessary for required symbols, but must also consider that required symbols might be satisfied by multiple nodes; we can choose which to keep. The \"grammar-valid\" might require that if we remove a node, all its children must also be removed (unless they have other parents?). In a tree, each node has exactly one parent. So deleting a node leaves its children orphaned; they cannot be retained unless we reattach them to the parent of the deleted node. The problem might allow reattachment as a \"replacement\" operation: replace a node with its child (i.e., delete the parent and keep the child at same depth?). That is a grammar production: if you have a production A -> B C, and you replace A with B, you keep B and discard C. That would be a valid reduction. So we could replace n2 (symbol s2) with n4 (symbol s1). That would remove n2 and keep n4. Then n4 becomes child of root with depth 1? Actually if n2 is replaced by n4, then n4 moves up to depth 1 (parent becomes root). That's allowed if the grammar allows replacing s2 with s1? But we don't have grammar rules. However, the required_symbols list suggests that \"s1\" is required, so replacing a node with s1 may be beneficial.\n\nAlternatively, we could replace n1 with n6? n6 has s0, not required. So not useful.\n\nSo the decision is: for each removable node, we can either delete it (remove subtree? or keep children?), replace it with one of its children (keeping only that child), or replace it with a sequence of its children? The term \"replacement candidates\" suggests we can replace a node with a subset of its children, as long as the resulting sequence of symbols is valid according to some grammar. But grammar not given.\n\nMaybe the grammar is inferred from the tree: the children of a node form a sequence of symbols. Replacing a node with its children means that the parent now has those children directly, which might be valid if the grammar's production for the parent allows that sequence. But we don't have grammar rules.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, perhaps the intended solution is simple: retain all nodes that are not removable, and also retain any removable nodes that are required to satisfy the required_symbols (i.e., if a required symbol only appears on removable nodes, keep them). But in our case, s1 appears on non-removable n1, so we can drop all removable nodes. That matches earlier guess.\n\nBut we need to consider that non-removable nodes must be retained, and we cannot remove them. So retained_node_ids will include all non-removable nodes. Then removed_node_ids will include all removable nodes that we decide to remove. But we must also ensure that after removal, the tree is still valid (each retained node's parent is also retained). If we remove a node, its children cannot be retained unless their parent is retained. So if a child is non-removable, we cannot remove its parent if it is the only parent. In the tree, parent relationships: root is non-removable. Its children: n1 (non-removable), n2 (removable), n3 (non-removable). If we remove n2, its child n4 (removable) can also be removed; no problem. n4 is removable, so we can delete it. But if n4 were non-removable, we would have to keep n2 to keep n4. In our case, n4 is removable, so fine. Similarly, n3 is non-removable, its child n5 is non-removable, so we must keep both. n1 is non-removable, its child n6 is removable, so we can remove n6. So the retained nodes are exactly all non-removable nodes. That yields retained: root, n1, n3, n5. Removed: n2, n4, n6. That seems consistent with \"retain all non-removable nodes, remove all removable nodes that are not required\". Since required_symbols are already satisfied by non-removable nodes, we can remove all removable.\n\nBut is there any scenario where a required symbol only appears on removable nodes? In these fixtures, required_symbols are \"root\" (on non-removable root) and \"s1\" (on non-removable n1 and removable n4). So no. In general, we may need to keep some removable nodes to satisfy required symbols. The algorithm should: for each required symbol, ensure at least one node with that symbol is retained. Among those, we may prefer non-removable if exists. If only removable, keep at least one. Also we must maintain tree connectivity: if we keep a node, all its ancestors must be kept. So we cannot delete an ancestor if a descendant is retained. Therefore, to retain a node, we must retain its entire ancestor chain to root. That means if a required symbol appears only on a removable node deep, we may need to keep it and possibly some ancestors that are also removable. So the algorithm: start with mandatory retained set: all non-removable nodes (since we cannot remove them). Then, for each required symbol, if not already satisfied by a retained node (i.e., no retained node has that symbol), we need to select at least one node with that symbol that is currently not retained (must be removable), and retain it along with all its ancestors (which may be removable or not). This ensures the symbol is present and tree is connected. After that, we can remove all remaining removable nodes that are not retained.\n\nThis seems plausible for a \"grammar-guided node reduction\" where the grammar implicitly defines required symbols (like start symbols or nonterminals that must appear). The interestingness oracle is that required symbols must be present. The reduction is grammar-valid because we only delete or replace subtrees, but here we are just removing nodes and keeping ancestors. That might not be full grammar-valid replacement but perhaps a simplified version.\n\nWe also have utility values, but not used in this decision. Could be used to decide which removable node to keep if multiple options. For example, if required symbol appears on multiple removable nodes, we might keep the one with highest utility, and also its ancestors, possibly replacing other ancestors? But ancestors also have utility. The objective might be to maximize total utility of retained nodes given the constraints. However, the output only asks for removed/retained ids, not any score. So the algorithm must produce a deterministic output for each case. Maybe the utility is used to break ties? But fixtures have different utilities, yet same decision? In case 0, n4 utility 0.665, n1 non-removable; but we keep n1. No tie. In case 1, n4 utility 0.272, n1 0.500, still keep n1. In case 2, n4 0.803, n1 0.416; here n4 has higher utility than n1! If we were maximizing utility, we might replace n1 with n4? But n1 is non-removable, so we cannot remove it. So n1 must stay. But if we could replace n1 with something? No, non-removable. So even though n4 has higher utility, we cannot remove n1. So utility doesn't change decision.\n\nBut consider a hypothetical where required symbol appears on non-removable and removable, but we might want to keep the removable instead to allow removal of ancestor that is also removable? For instance, if required symbol appears on a removable node under a removable ancestor, and there is another non-removable node with same symbol, we might still remove the removable path entirely because the symbol is already covered elsewhere. So utility doesn't matter.\n\nGiven the fixture, all cases have same structure, so output likely identical. Thus the algorithm described (retain all non-removable, retain minimal additional removable to satisfy required symbols, remove rest) yields same output for all three.\n\nBut we must implement solve(case) based on payload only. So let's write Python code.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Create dict mapping node id to node object.\n2. Determine set of non-removable node ids (retained by default).\n3. For each node, build parent relationship.\n4. Determine set of required symbols from case[\"required_symbols\"].\n5. For each required symbol, check if any retained node (from non-removable set) already has that symbol. If yes, fine.\n6. For any required symbol not satisfied, we need to select a removable node with that symbol to retain. There might be multiple. Choose one (e.g., the one with highest utility? Or first? To be deterministic, we can sort by id or utility descending. But need consistency. We'll sort by utility descending then by id to break ties. But beware that if we choose a node, its ancestors must also be retained (they might be removable or non-removable). So we add that node and all its ancestors (up to root) to the retained set. Note: root is non-removable so already in retained.\n7. After ensuring all required symbols are covered, we have final retained set. removed set is all node ids not in retained set.\n8. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": list(removed), \"retained_node_ids\": list(retained)}. Order? Not specified, but we can sort for determinism. Probably sorted by id or insertion order.\n\nImportant: We must not mutate case. Also ensure we handle cases where a required symbol might appear on a non-removable node that is not retained because its ancestor is removable? No, we retain all non-removable by default, so if a non-removable node has required symbol, it's already in retained. But what if a non-removable node is not reachable from root due to removable ancestors? In a tree, parent is defined. If a node's parent is removable, the node is still part of the tree. But if we remove the parent, the node becomes orphaned; if the node is non-removable, then we cannot remove the parent? Actually, we cannot remove a non-removable node, but we can remove its parent if parent is removable? But then the non-removable child would be disconnected. The problem likely requires that the final tree must be connected (i.e., each retained node's parent must also be retained). Therefore, we must ensure that if we retain a node, all its ancestors are retained. So our algorithm should enforce that: any node we retain (including non-removable) implies its parent must be retained. Non-removable nodes: they might have removable ancestors. If we retain a non-removable node, we must also retain its ancestors, even if removable, to keep connectivity. So our initial \"all non-removable nodes\" set is not valid if any non-removable node has a removable ancestor that we might remove. Therefore, we must consider that retaining non-removable nodes forces retention of their ancestors. So the correct approach is: start with a set of mandatory nodes: all non-removable nodes plus root. Then propagate upwards: for each node in retained, its parent must be retained (if parent exists). This will add removable ancestors. Then check required symbols. For required symbols not covered, add a suitable node and its ancestors.\n\nBut in the fixtures, non-removable nodes: root, n1, n3, n5. Their ancestors: root is itself. n1's parent is root, already retained. n3's parent root, retained. n5's parent n3, retained (since n3 non-removable). So no additional removable ancestors. So initial retained set remains exactly those four. That matches earlier.\n\nGeneral algorithm:\n\n- Build parent dict: for each node, parent id (null for root).\n- Build children dict for traversal maybe not needed.\n- Initialize retained = set()\n- For each node, if not removable, add it to retained.\n- Add root if not already (root might be removable? Probably not, but in case; root is always non-removable in fixtures).\n- Propagate: for each node in retained (list), ensure its parent is in retained; repeat until stable. Use a stack/queue: for each node, while parent exists and parent not in retained, add parent and continue.\n- After propagation, we have base retained set that includes all non-removable and their ancestor chain.\n- Now check required symbols: for each symbol in required_symbols, check if any node in retained has that symbol. If no, then we need to add a node with that symbol that is not yet retained. The node must be removable (since non-removable are already in retained). We choose one; need to ensure that adding it and its ancestors does not conflict with anything. We'll pick the one with highest utility (since objective might be to maximize utility, but we are just implementing a deterministic rule). If multiple, tie-break by id. Then add that node and its ancestors to retained. After adding, re-check all required symbols (since adding ancestors might add new symbols that satisfy other required symbols). Loop until all required symbols are satisfied or no suitable node (should not happen).\n- Finally, removed = set(all node ids) - retained.\n- Convert sets to sorted lists (by id) for consistent output.\n\nWe must handle the case where a required symbol might be satisfied by multiple nodes but only some have benefits. We'll just pick one per symbol when unsatisfied.\n\nEdge case: What if a required symbol can only be satisfied by a node that is already in retained due to ancestor propagation, but we didn't record it? We check after each addition.\n\nThis should produce deterministic output.\n\nNow, we need to write solve(case) that takes the payload (with \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\") and returns dict with keys removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nCheck: The payload may have other fields like \"_case_nonce\", but we ignore.\n\nImplement:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    req_syms = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    # Build parent mapping\n    parent = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        parent[n[\"id\"]] = n[\"parent\"]  # None for root\n    # Identify root node (parent=None)\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is None:\n            root_id = n[\"id\"]\n            break\n    # Initialize retained set\n    retained = set()\n    # Add all non-removable nodes\n    for n in nodes:\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            retained.add(n[\"id\"])\n    # Ensure root is included (if removable? but root is non-removable in fixtures; still safe)\n    retained.add(root_id)\n    # Propagate ancestors for all retained nodes\n    changed = True\n    while changed:\n        changed = False\n        # Need to iterate over a copy because we might add during loop\n        current_retained = list(retained)\n        for nid in current_retained:\n            p = parent[nid]\n            if p is not None and p not in retained:\n                retained.add(p)\n                changed = True\n    # Now ensure required symbols\n    # We'll check and add as needed\n    # We may need multiple passes because adding a node might satisfy multiple symbols\n    # We'll loop until we don't add anything new\n    while True:\n        uncovered = [sym for sym in req_syms if not any(node_map[nid][\"symbol\"] == sym for nid in retained)]\n        if not uncovered:\n            break\n        # Choose a symbol to satisfy (first uncovered)\n        sym = uncovered[0]\n        # Find all nodes with this symbol that are not already in retained\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\"symbol\"] == sym and n[\"id\"] not in retained]\n        if not candidates:\n            # Should not happen, but break to avoid infinite loop\n            break\n        # Choose candidate: highest utility, then id for tie\n        candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[\"utility\"], x[\"id\"]))\n        chosen = candidates[0]\n        # Add chosen and its ancestors\n        # We'll traverse from chosen up to root, adding all\n        node = chosen[\"id\"]\n        while node is not None:\n            if node not in retained:\n                retained.add(node)\n            node = parent[node]  # parent[None]? parent of root is None, so loop ends\n    # After all, compute removed\n    all_ids = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removed = all_ids - retained\n    # Return sorted lists\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": sorted(removed),\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(retained)\n    }\n\nWe must ensure we handle that case[\"required_symbols\"] is a list.\n\nWe should test on fixture case 0.\n\nCase 0: non-removable: root, n1, n3, n5. Propagate ancestors: none added (parents already in retained). req_syms: root and s1. root is present (root). s1 present? n1 has s1, yes. So uncovered empty. retained = {root, n1, n3, n5}. removed = {n2, n4, n6}. Sorted: removed: [\"n2\",\"n4\",\"n6\"]; retained: [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. That matches expectation.\n\nFor other cases, same.\n\nBut what if there is a scenario where a non-removable node's parent is removable and not yet in retained? Our propagation step adds it. So that removable ancestor becomes retained. For example, if n1 were